= Solution
{parent=1}

Perpendicular <vectors> have zero <dot product>. Here $\mathbf u\cdot\mathbf v=3\cdot4-2c$, so $12-2c=0$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{c=6}.
$$

= 2
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2}

The numerator is the <derivative> of the denominator. Using the <chain rule> in reverse gives the <antiderivative>
$$
\boxed{\ln|x^2+3x-2|+C}.
$$
The <absolute value> makes this real on every interval where the denominator is nonzero. The integration constant may be chosen independently on the intervals separated by $x=(-3\pm\sqrt{17})/2$.

= 3
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=3}

The <quotient rule> simplifies the <derivative> to
$$
y'=\frac{7(x+1)(x+5)}{(x-1)^2(x+2)^2}.
$$
Its zeros are $-5$ and $-1$, both in the domain. Substitution gives the two <stationary points>
$$
\boxed{(-5,25/9),\qquad(-1,9)}.
$$
The first is a <local maximum> and the second a <local minimum>, since the numerator of $y'$ changes sign from positive to negative at $-5$ and from negative to positive at $-1$. The denominator of the original <rational function> vanishes at
$$
\boxed{x=-2,\qquad x=1}.
$$
The numerator is nonzero at both values, so these discontinuities are poles, not removable holes.

= 4
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=4}

Direct <matrix multiplication> gives
$$
A^2=\begin{pmatrix}9&8\\16&17\end{pmatrix},\qquad
4A+5I=\begin{pmatrix}9&8\\16&17\end{pmatrix}.
$$
Subtracting verifies $\boxed{A^2-4A-5I=0}$. This is also the <Cayley-Hamilton theorem> for the <characteristic polynomial> $\lambda^2-4\lambda-5$.

= 5
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=5}

Rationalize with the <complex conjugate> $4-3i$:
$$
\frac{5-2i}{4+3i}=\frac{(5-2i)(4-3i)}{4^2+3^2}
=\frac{14-23i}{25}.
$$
Thus the <real part> and <imaginary part> are
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{Re}=\frac{14}{25},\qquad\operatorname{Im}=-\frac{23}{25}}.
$$

= 6
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=6}

For $f(x)=\sqrt x$, the first two <derivatives> at one are $f'(1)=1/2$ and $f''(1)=-1/4$. The <Taylor expansion> begins
$$
\boxed{\sqrt x=1+\frac12(x-1)-\frac18(x-1)^2+O((x-1)^3)}.
$$
All three displayed terms are nonzero terms of the expansion about one.

= 7
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=7}

This is a <separable differential equation>. Multiply by $e^{-y}$ and integrate:
$$
-e^{-y}=\frac12\ln(1+x^2)+C.
$$
The <initial condition> gives $C=-1$. Hence
$$
\boxed{y(x)=-\ln\left(1-\frac12\ln(1+x^2)\right)}.
$$
For a real solution the outer <logarithm> requires a positive argument. The maximal interval containing the initial point is therefore $\boxed{|x|<\sqrt{e^2-1}}$. At either endpoint $y$ diverges, so the real solution cannot continue through it with a finite value.

= 8
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=8}

Write $z=x+iy$. The <complex modulus> condition is equivalent to $(x-1)^2+y^2=4$. Thus the locus is a **<circle> of radius two centred at $(1,0)$** in the <complex plane>. Its extreme points are $(-1,0)$, $(3,0)$, and $(1,\pm2)$.

\Image[paper-2-complex-circle.png]
{title=Circle in the complex plane with centre one and radius two}

= 9
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=9}

The <derivative> is $dy/dx=3x^2$, giving slope twelve at $x=2$. The point on the <curve> is $(2,9)$. The point-slope equation of the <tangent line> is consequently
$$
\boxed{y-9=12(x-2),\qquad y=12x-15}.
$$

= 10
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=10}

In <Cartesian coordinates>, the <divergence> uses the matching coordinate for each component:
$$
\boxed{\nabla\cdot\mathbf F=\partial_xy+\partial_yz+\partial_zx=0}.
$$
Each of these three <partial derivatives> vanishes.

= 11
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=11}
{scope}

= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The <gradient> part contributes zero around a closed path by the <fundamental theorem for line integrals>:
$$
\oint_{\Gamma_1}3\nabla\phi\cdot d\mathbf x=0.
$$
Along the parametrization, $x^2+z^2=1$, so the first two components of $\mathbf w$ vanish wherever the printed expression is defined. The remaining component gives $\mathbf w\cdot d\mathbf x=x\,dz=\cos^2\theta\,d\theta$. Thus
$$
\boxed{\oint_{\Gamma_1}(3\mathbf v+\mathbf w)\cdot d\mathbf x
=\int_0^{2\pi}\cos^2\theta\,d\theta=\pi}.
$$
**The printed field needs a qualification:** $\operatorname{arctanh}(x^2)$ is undefined at the isolated parameters where $x^2=1$, so $0\operatorname{arctanh}(1)$ cannot literally be evaluated. The result above is the <improper line integral>, or equivalently the <integral> of the continuous extension of the restricted field along the <curve>. That restricted field tends to $(0,0,\cos\theta)$ at each omitted parameter. No extension of the full field to a neighbourhood is being assumed.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The <curl of a gradient> vanishes because the mixed second <partial derivatives> of the smooth <function> $\phi$ agree. Therefore $\nabla\times\mathbf v=\nabla\times\nabla\phi=0$ everywhere, and
$$
\boxed{\int_{\Gamma_2}(\nabla\times\mathbf v)\cdot d\mathbf x=0}.
$$
Unlike part (i), this integrand does not involve the problematic auxiliary field.

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Where $\mathbf A=|\mathbf v|^2\mathbf w$ is twice continuously differentiable, the identity <divergence of a curl is zero> gives $\nabla\cdot(\nabla\times\mathbf A)=0$. All regular portions of the parameter interval lie in this region, hence
$$
\boxed{\int_{\Gamma_2}\nabla\cdot[\nabla\times(|\mathbf v|^2\mathbf w)]\,ds=0}
$$
as an <improper line integral>. Besides the $\operatorname{arctanh}(x^2)$ endpoint problem from (i), the <curve> touches $y=1$ at $\theta=\pi/2$, where the printed real $\arcsin y$ expression has no smooth open neighbourhood. The identity applies on the intervening open segments, and its zero value has a unique continuous extension along the <curve>. A literal classical integrand at every parameter is not supplied by the printed field.

= b
{parent=11}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Writing $r^2=x^2+y^2+z^2$, the <gradient> is $\mathbf v=2e^{r^2}(x,y,z)$. Its <divergence>, equivalently the <Laplacian> of $\phi$, is
$$
\nabla\cdot\mathbf v=(6+4r^2)e^{r^2}.
$$
On the unit <sphere> this <scalar> is the constant $10e$. The lower <hemisphere> has <surface area> $2\pi$, so
$$
\boxed{\int_S(\nabla\cdot\mathbf v)\,dS=20\pi e}.
$$
This is a <scalar> <surface integral> of the divergence, not the electric- or vector-field flux <integral>. There is no normal component to insert.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The <scalar> integrand still equals $10e$ throughout the unit <sphere>, and the upper <hemisphere> has the same <surface area> $2\pi$. Excluding its equator changes no <area>. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\int_{S'}(\nabla\cdot\mathbf v)\,dS=20\pi e}.
$$
There is no sign reversal between hemispheres because this <scalar> <surface integral> has no choice of normal orientation.

= 12
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=12}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Using the physics convention for <spherical coordinates>, the <polar angle> $\theta$ is measured from positive $z$ and the <azimuth> $\phi$ from positive $x$ toward positive $y$. The <Cartesian coordinates> are
$$
\boxed{x=r\sin\theta\cos\phi,\qquad y=r\sin\theta\sin\phi,\qquad z=r\cos\theta}.
$$

= b
{parent=12}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Hold $r$ fixed and differentiate the position <vector> $\mathbf X(\theta,\phi)$. Its coordinate tangent <vectors> are
$$
\mathbf X_\theta=r(\cos\theta\cos\phi,\cos\theta\sin\phi,-\sin\theta),\qquad
\mathbf X_\phi=r(-\sin\theta\sin\phi,\sin\theta\cos\phi,0).
$$
Their <dot product> is zero, and their squared <Euclidean norms> are $r^2$ and $r^2\sin^2\theta$. To first order, the displacement is $\delta\mathbf X=\mathbf X_\theta\delta\theta+\mathbf X_\phi\delta\phi+O((|\delta\theta|+|\delta\phi|)^2)$. Squaring gives
$$
\boxed{\delta s^2\simeq r^2(\delta\theta^2+\sin^2\theta\,\delta\phi^2)}.
$$
This is the <line element on a sphere>. The chord distance and the short surface <arc length> agree to the order retained here.

= c
{parent=12}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

A <plane> through the origin has equation $lx+my+nz=0$ for some nonzero normal $(l,m,n)$. Dividing that normal by its <Euclidean norm> makes $l^2+m^2+n^2=1$ without changing the <plane>. Substitute the <spherical coordinates> from (a) and cancel the nonzero radius:
$$
\boxed{l\sin\theta\cos\phi+m\sin\theta\sin\phi+n\cos\theta=0,\qquad
l^2+m^2+n^2=1}.
$$
Its intersection with the <sphere> is the desired <great circle>; changing the sign of all three coefficients describes the same <circle>.

= d
{parent=12}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

At the first endpoint, the <Cartesian coordinates> are $(r,0,0)$, so its <great circle> must have $l=0$. At the second endpoint, the <plane> condition gives $m\sin\theta_0\sin\phi_0+n\cos\theta_0=0$. Since $\cos\theta_0>0$, an equivalent regular <plane> equation is
$$
\boxed{\cos\theta_0\sin\theta\sin\phi-\sin\theta_0\sin\phi_0\cos\theta=0}.
$$
Where $\cos\theta\ne0$, divide by $\cos\theta_0\cos\theta$ to obtain
$$
\boxed{\tan\theta\sin\phi=\tan\theta_0\sin\phi_0}.
$$
The regular implicit equation includes the equatorial first endpoint; the tangent form is undefined there and must be read by a limit. The shorter connecting arc of this <great circle> is the shortest path. The endpoints are not antipodal, so this shorter arc is unique. For example, the central angle is $\delta=\arccos(\sin\theta_0\cos\phi_0)$, giving <spherical distance> $r\delta$.

= 13
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=13}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

For $x>0$, the exponential definitions of the <hyperbolic functions> give $\cosh x-\sinh x=e^{-x}>0$ and $\sinh x>0$. Taking reciprocals of these positive quantities proves $1/\cosh x<1/\sinh x$. Also $\cosh x>1$ for $x>0$, so division by the positive $\sinh x$ gives $1/\sinh x<\cosh x/\sinh x$. Combining,
$$
\boxed{\frac1{\cosh x}<\frac1{\sinh x}<\coth x\qquad(x>0)}.
$$

= b
{parent=13}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The specified <unit vector> is $\mathbf u=(\cos(\pi/3),\sin(\pi/3))=(1/2,\sqrt3/2)$; the positive $y$ component selects that direction. The <gradient> is $\nabla f=e^{xy}(y,x)$, giving $(0,-2)$ at the point. The <directional derivative> is therefore
$$
\boxed{D_{\mathbf u}f=\nabla f\cdot\mathbf u=-\sqrt3}.
$$

= c
{parent=13}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Since $f>0$, a level $f=c$ can occur only for $c>0$. Taking its <natural logarithm> gives $xy=\ln c$. Thus $c>1$ produces rectangular <hyperbolas> in the first and third quadrants, $0<c<1$ produces them in the second and fourth quadrants, and $c=1$ consists of both coordinate axes.

The <gradient> $e^{xy}(y,x)$ vanishes only at $(0,0)$, where $f=1$. The <Hessian matrix> there is $\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\1&0\end{pmatrix}$, with negative <determinant>. Equivalently, $f(x,x)>1$ while $f(x,-x)<1$ for nonzero small $x$. Hence the only <stationary point> is a **<saddle point> at $(0,0,1)$** on the graph; its location in the contour <plane> is the origin.

\Image[paper-2-exponential-contours.png]
{title=Contours of the exponential of xy, showing the unique saddle at the origin}

= 14
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=14}
{scope}

= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Expand the <determinant> along the first row:
$$
\det A=(-2)(-12)-2(-6)+(-3)(-3)=24+12+9.
$$
Thus $\boxed{\det A=45}$. The signs in the middle cofactor account for its positive contribution.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The <characteristic polynomial>, using the convention $\det(\lambda I-A)$, is
$$
p(\lambda)=\lambda^3+\lambda^2-21\lambda-45
=(\lambda-5)(\lambda+3)^2.
$$
Its zeros give the <eigenvalues>, including <algebraic multiplicity>:
$$
\boxed{5,\qquad-3,\qquad-3}.
$$
Their product is $45$ and their sum is $-1$, agreeing with the <determinant> and <trace> of the <matrix>.

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

For $\lambda=5$, the <eigenvalue equation> reduces to a one-dimensional <eigenspace> spanned by $(1,2,-1)$. For $\lambda=-3$, the rows of $A+3I$ are multiples of $(1,2,-3)$, so its <eigenspace> is the <plane> $x+2y-3z=0$. One possible set of three independent <eigenvectors> is
$$
\boxed{\mathbf e_1=(1,2,-1)\quad(\lambda=5),\qquad
\mathbf e_2=(-2,1,0),\quad\mathbf e_3=(3,0,1)\quad(\lambda=-3)}.
$$
Every <eigenvector> for $-3$ is a nonzero <linear combination> of the last two. Their two-dimensional <eigenspace> shows that the repeated root is not defective: the <matrix> is <diagonalizable>.

= iv
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

For the particular <eigenvectors> chosen in (iii), the <dot products> are
$$
\mathbf e_1\cdot\mathbf e_2=0,\qquad
\mathbf e_1\cdot\mathbf e_3=2,\qquad
\mathbf e_2\cdot\mathbf e_3=-6.
$$
Thus **only $\mathbf e_1$ and $\mathbf e_2$ are <orthogonal>** for this choice. The <matrix> is not symmetric, so distinct <eigenvalues> alone do not imply <orthogonality>. Another basis within the repeated <eigenspace> may change which chosen <vectors> are perpendicular; the dot products above specify the convention completely.

= b
{parent=14}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The <transpose> reverses product order, so $(CB)^{\mathsf T}=B^{\mathsf T}C^{\mathsf T}$. If $B$ is an <orthogonal matrix>, then $B^{-1}=B^{\mathsf T}$ and the given identity becomes
$$
B^{\mathsf T}(C^{\mathsf T}+C)=0.
$$
Multiply on the left by $B$ to obtain $C^{\mathsf T}+C=0$. Therefore **$C$ is antisymmetric**, so it is an <antisymmetric matrix>, namely $\boxed{C^{\mathsf T}=-C}$.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Take <determinants> of $(CB)^{\mathsf T}=-B^{-1}C$. In order three, multiplication of a <matrix> by minus one changes its <determinant>'s sign. Hence
$$
\det C\,\det B=-\frac{\det C}{\det B}.
$$
The assumption $\det(B^{\mathsf T})\ne0$ is the same as $\det B\ne0$, so multiplication by $\det B$ gives
$$
\bigl((\det B)^2+1\bigr)\det C=0.
$$
Since the <matrices> are real, the first factor is strictly positive. Thus $\boxed{\det C=0}$, proving that $C$ is a <singular matrix>. This is <singularity forced by an odd-dimensional transpose-inverse identity>; no <orthogonality> of $B$ was used.

= 15
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=15}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

First solve for the <velocity> components. The <integrating factor> $e^{\gamma t}$ gives
$$
\dot x=V_{0x}e^{-\gamma t},\qquad
\dot z=\left(V_{0z}+\frac g\gamma\right)e^{-\gamma t}-\frac g\gamma.
$$
Integrating once more and imposing the initial position gives
$$
\boxed{x(t)=\frac{V_{0x}}\gamma(1-e^{-\gamma t}),\qquad
z(t)=\frac{V_{0z}+g/\gamma}\gamma(1-e^{-\gamma t})-\frac g\gamma t}.
$$
These formulas describe <projectile motion with linear drag>. Differentiation checks both second-order <differential equations> and both initial velocities. At large positive <time>, the horizontal <velocity> tends to zero and the vertical <velocity> tends to the terminal value $-g/\gamma$.

= b
{parent=15}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

If $V_{0x}\ne0$, the first formula in (a) gives $e^{-\gamma t}=1-\gamma x/V_{0x}$ and
$$
t=-\frac1\gamma\ln\left(1-\frac{\gamma x}{V_{0x}}\right).
$$
Substitute this and $1-e^{-\gamma t}=\gamma x/V_{0x}$ into $z(t)$:
$$
\boxed{z(x)=\left(\frac{V_{0z}}{V_{0x}}+\frac{g}{\gamma V_{0x}}\right)x
+\frac{g}{\gamma^2}\ln\left(1-\frac{\gamma x}{V_{0x}}\right)}.
$$
For finite physical times $t\geq0$, the domain is $0\leq\gamma x/V_{0x}<1$, valid for either sign of $V_{0x}$. If $V_{0x}=0$, the trajectory is vertical, $x(t)=0$, and in general no single-valued graph $z(x)$ describes it. As a consistency check, the logarithmic expansion at fixed $x$ cancels the apparent $1/\gamma$ terms and gives $z\to(V_{0z}/V_{0x})x-gx^2/(2V_{0x}^2)$ as $\gamma\to0$, the drag-free <parabola>.

= 16
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=16}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Use the <integration by substitution> $x=\sin\theta$, with $-\pi/6\leq\theta\leq\pi/6$. On this interval $\cos\theta>0$, so it cancels the positive denominator <square root>. The transformed <integral> is
$$
\frac12\int_{-\pi/6}^{\pi/6}\sqrt{1-\frac49\sin^2\theta}\,d\theta.
$$
Its integrand is even. Therefore the answer in the PDF's modulus-amplitude convention for the <elliptic integral of the second kind> is
$$
\boxed{E\left(\frac23,\frac\pi6\right)}.
$$
The modulus is $2/3$, not the squared parameter $4/9$.

= b
{parent=16}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Differentiating the parametrization gives $dx=a\cos\theta\,d\theta$ and $dy=-b\sin\theta\,d\theta$. Thus the <arc length> element is
$$
ds=\sqrt{a^2\cos^2\theta+b^2\sin^2\theta}\,d\theta
=a\sqrt{1-e^2\sin^2\theta}\,d\theta.
$$
All four quadrants give the same contribution. Consequently the <ellipse> perimeter is
$$
\boxed{L=a\int_0^{2\pi}\sqrt{1-e^2\sin^2\theta}\,d\theta
=4aE(e,\pi/2)}.
$$
The final factor is the <complete elliptic integral of the second kind>, evaluated at the <eccentricity of a conic> $e$.

= c
{parent=16}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

For small $e$, the <binomial series> gives the uniform expansion $\sqrt{1-e^2\sin^2\theta}=1-(e^2/2)\sin^2\theta+O(e^4)$. Since $\int_0^{2\pi}\sin^2\theta\,d\theta=\pi$, integration yields
$$
\boxed{L=2\pi a\left(1-\frac{e^2}{4}\right)+O(ae^4)
=\frac{\pi(3a^2+b^2)}{2a}+O\left(\frac{(a^2-b^2)^2}{a^3}\right)}.
$$
Also $b=a\sqrt{1-e^2}=a(1-e^2/2+O(e^4))$, so the familiar symmetric approximation $\boxed{L=\pi(a+b)+O(ae^4)}$ is equivalent to this order. This is the first correction in the <ellipse perimeter expansion near a circle>; the two approximate formulas need not agree at order $e^4$.

= 17
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=17}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Temporarily label the individual pieces. A random placement is a uniform <injection> of thirty-two distinct pieces into sixty-four squares, giving ${}_{64}P_{32}$ equally likely outcomes. For a colour-matching placement, the black pieces have ${}_{32}P_{16}$ available <injections>, and independently the white pieces have the same number within their disjoint set of squares. Therefore
$$
\boxed{P=\frac{({}_{32}P_{16})^2}{{}_{64}P_{32}}
=\frac{(32!)^3}{(16!)^2\,64!}}.
$$
Here ${}_nP_r=n!/(n-r)!$. Treating same-colour pieces as indistinguishable gives the same <probability>, because the common within-colour labeling factors cancel. The two colour choices in the favourable count occupy disjoint square sets; no independence assumption about unrestricted placements is required.

= b
{parent=17}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The lazy choice weights are $1/w$ and $1/(3w)$, in ratio three to one. Normalizing gives $\boxed{l=3/4}$. Let $Q$ denote selection of the first question and let $q=P(\text{Lazy}\mid Q)$. Its conditional <expected value>, divided by $m$, is
$$
\frac13q+(1-q)=\frac37,
$$
so $q=6/7$. If the overall lazy fraction is $p$, the <law of total probability> gives $P(Q)=(3/4)p+(1/2)(1-p)=(p+2)/4$. <Bayes' theorem> then gives
$$
\frac67=q=\frac{(3/4)p}{(p+2)/4}=\frac{3p}{p+2}.
$$
Solving $6(p+2)=21p$ yields $\boxed{p=4/5}$. The observed mean is conditioned on the chosen question, so it cannot be equated directly to the unconditional mean over all students.

= 18
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=18}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The integrand separates into a radial and an angular factor, so
$$
\int_0^{\pi/2}\int_0^R r^2\cos\theta\sin\theta\,dr\,d\theta
=\left[\frac{r^3}{3}\right]_0^R
\left[\frac{\sin^2\theta}{2}\right]_0^{\pi/2}
=\boxed{\frac{R^3}{6}}.
$$
The differential printed in the PDF is already $dr\,d\theta$. No further polar Jacobian is to be inserted into this given iterated <integral>.

= b
{parent=18}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The intersections satisfy $6x-x^2=2x+3$, or $(x-1)(x-3)=0$, so the bounded region has $1\leq x\leq3$. Over that interval the <parabola> is above the line, and their height difference is $(x-1)(3-x)$. Thus its <area> is
$$
\boxed{A=\int_1^3(4x-x^2-3)\,dx
=\left[2x^2-\frac{x^3}{3}-3x\right]_1^3=\frac43}.
$$
Using upper minus lower ensures a positive geometric <area>.

= c
{parent=18}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

The projection onto the $x$-$y$ <plane> is the <disk> $x^2+y^2\leq4$. In <polar coordinates>, the height is $4-r^2$ and the <polar area element> is $r\,dr\,d\theta$. The <volume> is therefore
$$
\boxed{\mathcal V=\int_0^{2\pi}\int_0^2(4-r^2)r\,dr\,d\theta
=2\pi\left[2r^2-\frac{r^4}{4}\right]_0^2=8\pi}.
$$
The radial factor here is required because this <integral> is being constructed from the geometric <volume>, unlike the already specified <integral> in (a).

= 19
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=19}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Put $c=2\alpha/\pi$. In the <right half-plane>,
$$
\Phi_u=-\frac{cv}{u^2+v^2},\quad
\Phi_v=\frac{cu}{u^2+v^2},\quad
\Phi_{uu}=\frac{2cuv}{(u^2+v^2)^2},\quad
\Phi_{vv}=-\frac{2cuv}{(u^2+v^2)^2}.
$$
The last two cancel, so $\boxed{\Phi_{uu}+\Phi_{vv}=0}$. The possible singular point $(0,0)$ lies outside the open domain $u>0$.

For $\alpha\ne0$, a <level curve> of value $\Phi_0$ has $v/u=\tan(\pi\Phi_0/(2\alpha))$. These are rays from the origin, restricted to the <right half-plane>. For positive $\alpha$, the values increase with angle from $-\alpha$ on the lower <boundary> to $+\alpha$ on the upper <boundary>. If $\alpha=0$, the <function> is identically zero. The left panel of the figure in (c) shows these ray-shaped <level curves>.

= b
{parent=19}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Let $D=(1-x)^2+y^2$. Direct <partial differentiation>, away from $(1,0)$, gives
$$
u_x=v_y=\frac{2((1-x)^2-y^2)}{D^2},\qquad
u_y=-v_x=-\frac{4(1-x)y}{D^2}.
$$
These are the <Cauchy-Riemann equations>. Equivalently, with $z=x+iy$, the analytic mapping is $u+iv=(1+z)/(1-z)$, the inverse <Cayley transform between the half-plane and disk>.

The supplied harmonicity result can also be checked by the <multivariable chain rule>. The <Cauchy-Riemann equations> imply $\Delta u=\Delta v=0$, $\nabla u\cdot\nabla v=0$, and $|\nabla u|^2=|\nabla v|^2$. Hence
$$
\Delta\Psi=(u_x^2+u_y^2)(\Phi_{uu}+\Phi_{vv})=0.
$$
This is <conformal invariance of harmonicity> for the given mapping.

= c
{parent=19}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Simplify the real component to $u=(1-x^2-y^2)/D$. Thus $u=0$ is the unit <circle> with $(1,0)$ excluded from the mapping's domain. Its upper semicircle has $v>0$ and its lower semicircle has $v<0$; the point $(-1,0)$ maps to $u=v=0$. Inside the <disk>, $u>0$, so the principal arctangent is unambiguous. Setting $\alpha=V$ gives
$$
\boxed{\Psi(x,y)=\frac{2V}{\pi}\arctan\frac{2y}{1-x^2-y^2},\qquad x^2+y^2<1}.
$$
The <Laplace equation> follows from (b). Approaching an open upper <boundary> arc makes the ratio tend to $+\infty$, giving $+V$; the lower arc gives $-V$. This solution is $V(2F-1)$ in terms of the <harmonic measure of a semicircle in a disk> $F$.

The <boundary> data jump at $(\pm1,0)$, so no path-independent continuous <boundary> value can be imposed at those two junctions. The displayed bounded <harmonic function> is the solution with the specified limits on the open arcs; those endpoint discontinuities are part of the idealized <capacitor> geometry.

